DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
       ggeett__mmeemmppoolliiccyy() retrieves the NUMA policy of the calling thread or of a
       memory address, depending on the setting of _f_l_a_g_s.

       A  NUMA  machine  has  different memory controllers with different dis‐
       tances to specific CPUs.  The memory policy  defines  from  which  node
       memory is allocated for the thread.

       If _f_l_a_g_s is specified as 0, then information about the calling thread’s
       default policy (as set by sseett__mmeemmppoolliiccyy(2)) is returned, in the buffers
       pointed to by _m_o_d_e and _n_o_d_e_m_a_s_k.  The value returned in these arguments
       may  be used to restore the thread’s policy to its state at the time of
       the call to ggeett__mmeemmppoolliiccyy() using sseett__mmeemmppoolliiccyy(2).  When _f_l_a_g_s  is  0,
       _a_d_d_r must be specified as NULL.

       If  _f_l_a_g_s specifies MMPPOOLL__FF__MMEEMMSS__AALLLLOOWWEEDD (available since Linux 2.6.24),
       the _m_o_d_e argument is ignored and the set of nodes (memories)  that  the
       thread  is  allowed  to  specify  in  subsequent  calls  to mmbbiinndd(2) or
       sseett__mmeemmppoolliiccyy(2) (in the absence of any  _m_o_d_e  _f_l_a_g_s)  is  returned  in
       _n_o_d_e_m_a_s_k.   It is not permitted to combine MMPPOOLL__FF__MMEEMMSS__AALLLLOOWWEEDD with ei‐
       ther MMPPOOLL__FF__AADDDDRR or MMPPOOLL__FF__NNOODDEE.

       If _f_l_a_g_s specifies MMPPOOLL__FF__AADDDDRR, then information is returned about  the
       policy  governing the memory address given in _a_d_d_r.  This policy may be
       different from the thread’s default policy if mmbbiinndd(2) or  one  of  the
       helper functions described in nnuummaa(3) has been used to establish a pol‐
       icy for the memory range containing _a_d_d_r.

       If  the  _m_o_d_e argument is not NULL, then ggeett__mmeemmppoolliiccyy() will store the
       policy mode and any optional _m_o_d_e _f_l_a_g_s of the requested NUMA policy in
       the location pointed to by this argument.  If  _n_o_d_e_m_a_s_k  is  not  NULL,
       then  the nodemask associated with the policy will be stored in the lo‐
       cation pointed to by this argument.  _m_a_x_n_o_d_e specifies  the  number  of
       node  IDs that can be stored into _n_o_d_e_m_a_s_k—that is, the maximum node ID
       plus one.  The value specified by _m_a_x_n_o_d_e is always rounded to a multi‐
       ple of _s_i_z_e_o_f_(_u_n_s_i_g_n_e_d _l_o_n_g_)_*_8.

       If _f_l_a_g_s specifies both MMPPOOLL__FF__NNOODDEE  and  MMPPOOLL__FF__AADDDDRR,  ggeett__mmeemmppoolliiccyy()
       will  return the node ID of the node on which the address _a_d_d_r is allo‐
       cated into the location pointed to by _m_o_d_e.  If no page  has  yet  been
       allocated  for  the  specified address, ggeett__mmeemmppoolliiccyy() will allocate a
       page as if the thread had performed a read (load) access  to  that  ad‐
       dress, and return the ID of the node where that page was allocated.

       If  _f_l_a_g_s  specifies MMPPOOLL__FF__NNOODDEE, but not MMPPOOLL__FF__AADDDDRR, and the thread’s
       current policy is  MMPPOOLL__IINNTTEERRLLEEAAVVEE  or  MMPPOOLL__WWEEIIGGHHTTEEDD__IINNTTEERRLLEEAAVVEE,  then
       ggeett__mmeemmppoolliiccyy()  will  return  in the location pointed to by a non‐NULL
       _m_o_d_e argument, the node ID of the next node that will be used  for  in‐
       terleaving  of internal kernel pages allocated on behalf of the thread.
       These allocations include pages for memory‐mapped files in process mem‐
       ory ranges mapped using the mmmmaapp(2) call with the MMAAPP__PPRRIIVVAATTEE flag  for
       read accesses, and in memory ranges mapped with the MMAAPP__SSHHAARREEDD flag for
       all accesses.

       Other flag values are reserved.

       For an overview of the possible policies see sseett__mmeemmppoolliiccyy(2).

